xanthian@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG (Kent Paul Dolan) writes:

>In article <596@lysator.liu.se> calle@lysator.liu.se (Calle Dybedahl) writes:

>> I don't think the myths specify how much Mjollnir weighs. I'm sure the
>> Eddas don't, but it may be mentioned somewhere else. Also, there's a
>> story about the hammer being stolen, so it can't be that heavy.

>Well, it rather helps to remember it was stolen by giants.  Might be
>portable by giants and still too heavy for mere humans to lift.

That's right, but I've alawys had the impression that it wasn't very
heavy. So I browsed through Snorri's Edda to see what it says. 
The only ones I have found who actually carries Mjollnir are Thor (of
course), Trym (the giant who stole it) and Brokk (the dwarf who made
it). Thor is Thor, Trym is a giant and Brokk a divine (sort of) smith,
so they may all be much stronger than ordinary mortals. I still don't
think the hammer is very heavy, though, based on this passage from the
Edda (translated by me from Swedish):
	"...gave he the hammer to Thor and said,
	 that with it he could strike as hard as he wished and crush
	 everything which was in the way,
	 and never would the hammer fail in the stroke or miss it's
	 target if he threw it, and no matter how far it flew it would
	 always return to the thrower's hand - and if one wanted one
	 could make it small enough to hide in the shirt.
	 But it had one flaw, and that was that the handle was a
	 little too short."


